A||||7||Gonzo_442||heater core||||5||
Z||000000||Gonzo_442||12-08-2000||07:18 PM||||it's come time to replace the core in my fiero. i have done this procedure on various other makes/models. however, i have in front of me here a chilton's manual, and a haynes manual. niether one specifies if you have to pull the dash out to do this. i've heard you have to. that true? also, the chilton manual claims you need to remove the passenger side front speaker (??) to do the job, yet the haynes manual mentions nothing about that. other than that, anyone have any tips/tricks on this job?<P>fwiw, its an 87gt, v6 w/ac.<P>along this same route, i'm flushing the coolant, replacing radiator/thermo caps, lower temp thermo, low temp fan switch.<P>--<BR>g||||reg||5
Z||000001||DXR_DAD||12-08-2000||08:36 PM||||Gonzo, I did mine a while ago, 86SE with ac<BR>The core comes out under the dash, about 4 little screws on the housing. One is at the top but you can get to it with out removing<BR>the speaker. Disconnect the hoses from under hood,remove the housing under pass.side and your done!<P>Here is some info that you will also need:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/005956.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/005956.html</A> <P>DXR_DAD<p>[This message has been edited by DXR_DAD (edited 12-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mark||12-08-2000||10:57 PM||||If you have a factory subwoofer, that has to come out also....pretty straightforward.  Pulling the speaker gains access to the upper screw.  All things considered, this was one of my easier Fiero repairs.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||12-08-2000||11:01 PM||||there's notes in my cave that may help some. <A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>The Fiero Cave</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||tgowens||12-09-2000||03:02 AM||||as they have all said - fairly easy - no, you do not have to remove the dash! - the hardest part is getting those two hoses off of the core inlets under the hood!!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Standard||12-09-2000||03:47 AM||||easy easy easy... until it comes time to seperate the hoses from the core.  After 13-14 years those things get one heck of a grip.  It helps if you make a small slit, but be careful 'cause there's not alot of extra hose.  Or try to get something between the hose and the pipe, and break all the corrosion/gum that's built up over the years.  Just try not to poke a hole in 'em.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||12-09-2000||10:15 AM||||Heater core is easy as mentioned above. Just follow the cooling system refill instructions in th Haynes manual, Not the Chiltons. You need to refill the system properly or else you will get air in the system and it could cause over-heating problems.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Gonzo_442||12-12-2000||02:48 AM||||thanks for the info everyone. getting to the hoses doesn't look like too much of a problem (yet  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> )<P>btw, a little tip i'll pass to those of you who have (had) trouble with the hoses. since in most cases (like mine now) the coolant is getting drained and flushed, sliding a small screwdriver down the side of a hose (carefully so as not to poke a hole in the hose) and then stick the squirt tube of a can of wd40 down it. but not all the way down past the part, maybe halfway.<BR>a quick shot, a little soak time, and they come off much easier.<BR>watch your eyes though, sometimes it will spray back at you. also watch how you hold onto it, i would imagine a screwdriver stuck in your wrist could be painful.<BR>on a throw away part (like a heater core) i use a steeper angle on the screwdriver and usually make a small gouge in the core tubes, to prevent damage to the inside of the hoses.||||reg||1
